{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>A holy convocation.</b> As its Targum: מערע קדיש — in it something holy occurs, a Yom Tov. <b>All labor shall not be done</b> — even through others. If so, we find that instructing a non-Jew is from the Torah, and we say in general that instructing a non-Jew is a shevut." "<b>In them.</b> In the two days, the first and the seventh." "<b>Only that which is eaten for every person.</b> Because it wrote here 'all labor,' it excluded from its category the thing that is eaten; therefore in all the festivals elsewhere it writes 'all labor of work you shall not do' — to exclude preparation of food for a person, which is not 'work.' But for Shabbat and Yom HaKippurim, where even preparation of food for a person is forbidden, it writes in them 'all labor you shall not do.'" "<b>For every person.</b> Even for an animal. And the fact that we say 'for you' and not for dogs — that is because it can forage for its food; it cannot for others — ת״ל אך. And even though it writes 'for you' and we say 'for you' and not for others — both are needed: for if only one were written, I would say on his own behalf he may not prepare, but to prepare extra on his behalf is permitted; therefore אך is needed."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The comment cites Rashi and the sages; the abbreviation חז״ק is resolved here as 'חז״ל' (the sages) based on context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:57:15.540663712Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}